      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The present study measured triglycerides (TG), total cholesterol (TC), HDL-cholesterol (HDL-C), LDL and LDL/HDL-C, TC/HDL-C and HDL-C/TC. 100 ratios in 261 men and women undergoing coronary arteriography. The results show that TG, TC, HDL-C and LDL levels are not related to the severity of coronary artery disease (CAD). The LDL/HDL-C, TC/HDL-C and HDL/TC.100 ratios are statistically related to the severity of CAD. The LDL/HDL-C ratio appeared to be the most sensitive index for reflecting CAD of mild severity with a predictive value of 83.3% at 76.6% specificity and at 77.7% sensitivity (cut-off: 3.20). Average values of LDL and HDL-C alone are not reliable indices of mild severity CAD."},
